<!-- ============ 01 ORIGIN ============ -->
<section class="sec stone" id="origin">
 <div class="wrap">
  <span class="lab">01 / The Origin Story</span>
  <h2>From a single question<br>to a conviction.</h2>
  <div class="rule"></div>
  <div class="nar">
   <div class="quote">
    <p>"Is there a service online where someone can sign up and either post a task to be performed, or requires physical presence for any legal execution or completion, for people who are not in that area? And obviously the opposite — to sign up to perform these tasks for the amount outlined by the task creator?"</p>
    <cite>— The Initial Inquiry</cite>
   </div>
   <p><strong>The answer was: partially, but not for legal execution.</strong> Platforms like TaskRabbit, Airtasker, and WeGoLook exist. They handle physical errands — assembling furniture, standing in queues, inspecting properties. But none of them touch the most valuable, most painful, most legally complex tasks: signing documents, lodging papers at government offices, executing legal mandates on behalf of someone who is not physically present.</p>
   <p>That gap was not an accident. It was a chasm created by fear, outdated law, and institutional inertia. And it was exactly where we needed to build.</p>

   <div class="tl">
    <div class="tl-i rv"><span class="ph">Chapter Two</span><h3>South Africa first. Then the world.</h3>
     <p>The decision was strategic, not sentimental. South Africa is the perfect pressure chamber:</p>
     <ul class="lst">
      <li><strong>High-friction bureaucracy:</strong> Home Affairs, traffic departments, and municipal offices are notorious for multi-hour queues.</li>
      <li><strong>Massive diaspora:</strong> Over one million South Africans live abroad, desperately needing trusted people back home to handle physical affairs.</li>
      <li><strong>Abundant labour supply:</strong> High unemployment means a ready pool of educated, articulate potential proxies.</li>
      <li><strong>Legal complexity:</strong> The intersection of ECTA, FICA, POPIA, and common law creates a rich environment for a trust-based solution.</li>
     </ul>
     <p style="margin-top:12px">If Presence can work in South Africa — with its load-shedding, its queues, its institutional resistance — <strong>it can work anywhere on earth.</strong></p>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-i rv"><span class="ph">Chapter Three</span><h3>The pivot: from gig app to enterprise software</h3>
     <p>The initial concept was a two-sided marketplace: consumers post tasks, proxies fulfill them. But research revealed a fatal flaw. <strong>Front-line clerks at banks, dealerships, and government offices will reject an unverified stranger every single time.</strong> The risk asymmetry is too great.</p>
     <p>The breakthrough came when we stopped asking, <em>"How do we convince the clerk to accept our proxy?"</em> and started asking, <em>"How do we make the proxy part of the clerk's own system?"</em> The answer was PresenceOS — not an app the proxy brings, but software the institution already trusts. An API. An integration. A button inside their own workflow that says: "Initiate Remote Proxy Execution."</p>
     <div class="quote" style="margin:16px 0 0"><p>"You are not building Uber. You are building Stripe. You are building the infrastructure that other businesses plug into."</p><cite>— Strategic Insight</cite></div>
    </div>
    <div class="tl-i rv"><span class="ph">Chapter Four</span><h3>The legal revelation</h3>
     <p>The most powerful discovery was this: <strong>the law already allows what we want to do.</strong> It is the institutions that haven't caught up. ECTA Section 13 validates electronic signatures. Advanced Electronic Signatures (AES) carry the same legal weight as wet ink. Courts accept video-commissioned affidavits. The CCMA accepts video link attendance as physical presence.</p>
     <p>The founder had already experienced this firsthand: buying a car with remote digital signatures, starting a new job with electronic contracts, receiving a credit card activated through software. <strong>The practice exists. The legislation supports it. What's missing is the trusted execution layer for physical presence.</strong> That is what Presence provides.</p>
    </div>
   </div>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ 02 PROBLEM ============ -->
<section class="sec ink grain" id="problem">
 <div class="wrap">
  <span class="lab m">02 / The Problem</span>
  <h2>The world moved online.<br>The law didn't.</h2>
  <div class="rule"></div>
  <div class="nar">
   <p class="lead" style="font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--paper)">Every day, millions of South Africans lose hours standing in queues at Home Affairs, traffic departments, and municipal offices. Expats in London and Dubai cannot sign a document for a property transaction in Cape Town. A car dealership in Sandton cannot close a deal because the buyer is in Thailand.</p>
   <p><strong style="color:var(--mango)">Physical presence is a tax on time.</strong> The technology to solve this exists. The legal framework — the Electronic Communications and Transactions Act (ECTA) of 2002 — explicitly supports it. Yet the system remains broken. Why? Because no one has built the trust layer that makes institutions comfortable accepting remote execution.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="stats" style="border-color:var(--mango)">
   <div class="stat"><b>1M+</b><span>SA diaspora abroad</span></div>
   <div class="stat alt"><b>R50B+</b><span>Annual vehicle finance</span></div>
   <div class="stat"><b>6M+</b><span>CCMA cases since 1996</span></div>
   <div class="stat alt"><b>340+</b><span>Municipalities in SA</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g3">
   <div class="card ink rv"><span class="num">01</span><h3>The Liability Chasm</h3>
    <p>For twenty years, the gig economy optimized the movement of things: a burger from a kitchen to a couch, a parcel from a warehouse to a doorstep. But it never solved the movement of <strong style="color:var(--paper)">authority</strong>. The moment a task required legal standing, institutional trust, or physical execution on behalf of someone else, the gig economy stopped.</p>
    <p>When a task requires a signature, a FICA verification, or a legal declaration, the gig worker becomes a liability. If they make a mistake, who is responsible? If they are rejected by a bank clerk, who argues the law? If they handle sensitive documents, who insures them?</p>
    <p>No platform wanted to answer these questions. So they built walled gardens of low-risk tasks. <strong style="color:var(--mango)">We decided to build the bridge instead.</strong></p>
   </div>
   <div class="card ink rv"><span class="num">02</span><h3>The Front-Line Reality</h3>
    <p><strong style="color:var(--paper)">The Old Way:</strong> A customer sends a gig worker to a bank branch with a letter. The clerk looks at the worker, looks at the letter, and says: "I need the account holder here in person. This is not enough." The worker leaves. The customer is frustrated. The task fails. The platform refunds the fee. <em>Result: Institutional friction wins.</em></p>
    <p><strong style="color:var(--mango)">The Presence Way:</strong> The Proxy arrives. They open the Presence app. The clerk sees a verified mandate, an Advanced Electronic Signature, and a live video link to the customer. The app generates a Chain of Custody certificate. The clerk processes the task because the software satisfies their compliance checklist. <em>Result: The mandate is executed.</em></p>
   </div>
   <div class="card ink rv"><span class="num">03</span><h3>The Statutory Lag</h3>
    <p>The most frustrating discovery in our research: <strong style="color:var(--paper)">the law already allows what we are doing.</strong> ECTA validates digital signatures. Courts accept video-commissioned affidavits. The CCMA accepts virtual attendance as physical presence. Banks and dealerships use remote signing software every day.</p>
    <p>The gap was never legal. <strong style="color:var(--mango)">The gap was operational.</strong> No one had built the software that puts these legal tools into the hands of a regular person standing in a government queue. Presence is that software.</p>
   </div>
  </div>
  <p style="text-align:center;margin-top:30px;font-family:'Space Mono';font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.3em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--mango)">This is not a niche. This is the infrastructure of a modern economy.</p>
