<!-- ============ 05 LEGAL ============ -->
<section class="sec" id="legal">
 <div class="wrap">
  <span class="lab">05 / The Legal Framework</span>
  <h2>Where the law says one thing,<br>and practice says another.</h2>
  <div class="rule"></div>
  <div class="nar">
   <p style="font-size:1.08rem">The Electronic Communications and Transactions Act (ECTA) 25 of 2002 is the legal bedrock of Presence. It explicitly validates electronic signatures.</p>
   <p><strong>Section 13(3):</strong> An electronic signature is not without legal force simply because it is digital.</p>
   <p><strong>Section 13(4):</strong> An Advanced Electronic Signature (AES) — accredited by bodies like LAWtrust or the SA Post Office — carries the exact same legal weight as a wet ink signature.</p>
   <p>This means: when a client signs through Presence using an AES, <strong>the institution cannot legally reject it simply because it is digital. The law is on our side.</strong></p>
   <div class="quote"><p>"I have done this with my vehicle when I purchased it. The dealership and the bank both sent me software that allowed remote signature. The same happened with a new job I started. The same happened with a new credit card."</p><cite>— Direct User Experience</cite></div>
   <p>This is not theoretical. This is not a loophole. <strong>This is established commercial practice.</strong> The question was never whether digital execution is legal. The question was: why hasn't someone built the trusted infrastructure for physical presence tasks? Presence answers that question.</p>
  </div>

  <span class="lab" style="margin-top:40px">Statutory Lag — The Gap Where the Opportunity Lives</span>
  <div class="grid g4">
   <div class="card stone rv"><h3>Affidavits</h3><p><strong>The Law:</strong> The 1963 Commissioners of Oaths Act requires physical presence.</p><p><strong>The Reality:</strong> Courts now routinely accept video-commissioned affidavits. The High Court in <em>Knop v Minister of Police</em> accepted virtual swearing. Practice has moved beyond the statute.</p></div>
   <div class="card stone rv"><h3>CCMA Hearings</h3><p><strong>The Law:</strong> Parties were historically required to attend in person.</p><p><strong>The Reality:</strong> The CCMA formally amended its rules post-2020. Video link attendance is now accepted as physical presence. This is codified practice.</p></div>
   <div class="card stone rv"><h3>FICA Verification</h3><p><strong>The Law:</strong> Face-to-face verification was historically required.</p><p><strong>The Reality:</strong> The Financial Intelligence Centre issued Guidance Notes allowing a "Risk-Based Approach." Biometric digital verification satisfies the requirement.</p></div>
   <div class="card stone rv"><h3>Vehicle Finance &amp; Employment</h3><p><strong>The Law:</strong> No specific law requires wet signatures for credit agreements or employment contracts.</p><p><strong>The Reality:</strong> Standard electronic signatures (clicking "I accept," signing on a tablet) are universally accepted. Banks, dealerships, and employers use them daily.</p></div>
  </div>

  <span class="lab" style="margin-top:40px">How Presence Executes Legally — Four Mechanisms</span>
  <div class="tl">
   <div class="tl-i rv"><span class="ph">Mechanism 1</span><h3>Advanced Electronic Signature (AES)</h3><p>When a client signs through Presence, the signature is cryptographically sealed via an AES provider. Under ECTA Section 13(4), this is legally presumed valid. The institution cannot reject it.</p></div>
   <div class="tl-i rv"><span class="ph">Mechanism 2</span><h3>The Amanuensis / Scribe Protocol</h3><p>For physical documents, the client gives verbal mandate on a live, recorded video call. The proxy signs their own name with "p.p." (per procurationem — on behalf of). The video recording is the legally binding proof of mandate.</p></div>
   <div class="tl-i rv"><span class="ph">Mechanism 3</span><h3>Remote Commissioning</h3><p>For affidavits, the proxy facilitates a secure video link between the client and a Commissioner of Oaths. The session is recorded, ID is verified live, and the client confirms they are not under duress. This satisfies post-2020 practice directives.</p></div>
   <div class="tl-i rv"><span class="ph">Mechanism 4</span><h3>Chain of Custody Certificate</h3><p>Every execution generates an immutable digital certificate: GPS coordinates, timestamp, video hash, biometric verification, and cryptographic signature. This is attached to the document and stored securely.</p></div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid g2">
   <div class="card ink rv"><h3 style="color:var(--mango)">Compliance — Non-Negotiable</h3>
    <p>Presence is built from the ground up to comply with the Protection of Personal Information Act (POPIA) and the Financial Intelligence Centre Act (FICA).</p>
    <ul class="lst">
     <li>All video recordings are encrypted at rest and in transit.</li>
     <li>Biometric data is processed with explicit consent and destroyed after verification.</li>
     <li>Proxies only see the information they need for the specific task.</li>
     <li>All data is stored on POPIA-compliant South African servers.</li>
     <li>Regular security audits and ISO 27001 alignment.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:12px;color:var(--mango);font-weight:700">Compliance is not a feature. It is the foundation.</p>
   </div>
   <div class="card rv" style="border-color:var(--ink);background:var(--stone)"><h3>The Red Lines — What We Never Allow</h3>
    <ul class="lst">
     <li><strong>No Cash / Financial Handling:</strong> Proxies never touch client money, buy on client cards, or pay municipal bills. All payments are digital and escrowed. FICA risk is too high.</li>
     <li><strong>No Legal Signatures Without POA:</strong> Proxies cannot sign contracts, affidavits, or deeds without a formal Special Power of Attorney.</li>
     <li><strong>No Scheduled Medications:</strong> Collecting Schedule 5+ drugs requires specific pharmacy authorization under the Pharmacy Act. We don't touch it.</li>
     <li><strong>No Driving Client Vehicles:</strong> Insurance liability in SA's high-accident environment makes this untenable. Proxies use their own transport.</li>
     <li><strong>No Unrepresented Legal Advice:</strong> Proxies execute mandates. They do not interpret the law or advise the customer.</li>
    </ul>
   </div>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

